      en: "Kabul, the capital of Afghanistan, sits at about 1,790 metres altitude in a narrow valley of the Hindu Kush and traces its origins back more than 3,500 years. Strung along the Kabul River, the city served for centuries as a key Silk Road junction connecting Persia, India and Central Asia. In the 16th century the Mughal emperor Babur chose Kabul as his favoured residence and laid out the celebrated Gardens of Babur, where his tomb still stands today. The Kabul metropolitan area is now home to more than four and a half million people, making it by far the largest urban centre in the country. Landmarks such as the Bala Hissar citadel, the Shah-Do Shamshira Mosque, the Darul Aman royal palace and the bustling Mandawi bazaar shape the cityscape. Despite decades of conflict, Kabul remains the seat of government, top universities and cultural institutions including the National Museum, which preserves the Bactrian gold and the famed finds from Bagram and Tillya Tepe.",
