      en: "Herat, in western Afghanistan, lies in the fertile valley of the Hari Rud River and is regarded as the cultural capital of the country. Known in antiquity as Aria, the city later became a major stop on the Silk Road between Persia and Central Asia. Its golden age came in the 15th century under the Timurids, when Queen Gauhar Shad and Sultan Husayn Bayqara fostered an artistic renaissance: miniature painting, calligraphy schools and poets such as Jami spread the city's fame far beyond Asia. The colossal tile-clad Friday Mosque, the five surviving minarets of the Mosalla complex and the mausoleum of Gauhar Shad still bear witness to this era. Above the city rises the Qala Ikhtyaruddin citadel, whose foundations are traditionally traced back to Alexander the Great. Herat is also a key trading hub for glass, silk and saffron, and home to a predominantly Persian-speaking Tajik population.",
